      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm hitting a constant CrashLoopBackOff on a microservice in our gke cluster. i've checked the basic logs but it's not super clear why it's failing right after startup. can you give me a diagnostic checklist to help me figure out if this is a resource issue, a liveness/readiness probe failure, or something in the config? \n\nhere's a snippet of the logs:\n[stderr] error: failed to parse config: unexpected EOF\n[stderr] connection refused: listening on port 8080 failed",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "1. Check if the microservice is properly a part of the cluster and the cluster has sufficient resources. Use the Kubernetes command `describe pod <pod_name>` to assess the pod's resource usage and any possible constraints.\n2. Review your deployment configuration, specifically liveness/readiness probes, and ensure they are correctly set up. Misconfigured probes may cause unnecessary restarts.\n3. Check for network issues such as firewall rules, networking between containers, or pods. Examine if there is access to port 8080, as indicated by your logs.\n4. Investigate possible misconfiguration as shown in your logs (\"failed to parse config: unexpected EOF\" and \"connection refused\"). A deeper look at both the config files of your microservice and GKE's configs can pinpoint issues here, especially for manifests, environment variables, and persistent volume configurations.",
